Alright, node_modules exists. Now, before the fix, let me write a failing regression test.

The tricky part: jsdom. Does jsdom implement `ResizeObserver`? jsdom doesn't natively implement `ResizeObserver` (as of jsdom 26, it's still unimplemented). Let me confirm: jsdom has no ResizeObserver. So in the test environment, `'ResizeObserver' in window` will be false, and chessground will fall back to the document-level `chessground.resize` event path.

Therefore, to test the RO leak, I need to mock `ResizeObserver` on window in the test (either via `vi.stubGlobal` or just assign `window.ResizeObserver = class ...`). Then:

Test 1 (leak on destroy): Create an RO mock that tracks instances and disconnect calls. Instantiate Chessground on a wrap, destroy, and assert that the RO was disconnected.

Test 2 (leak on redraw): Create an RO mock. Instantiate, then call `cg.redrawAll()` (or `cg.set({...})` that triggers a redraw), and assert that the previous RO was disconnected — i.e., that at any point the number of live (non-disconnected) ROs is at most 1.

To track "live" observers, the mock can push instances into an array and have `disconnect()` mark them.

Let me design the mock:

```ts
type ROEntry = {
  connected: boolean;
  targets: Element[];
};

let observers: ROEntry[] = [];

class MockResizeObserver {
  connected = true;
  targets: Element[] = [];
  constructor(cb: ResizeObserverCallback) {
    this.cb = cb;
    observers.push(this);
  }
  observe(el: Element) { this.targets.push(el); }
  unobserve(el: Element) { this.targets = this.targets.filter(t => t !== el); }
  disconnect() { this.connected = false; }
}
```

